<!--
  Directory.Build.targets  (CircleAI repo root)
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Android native-library bundling for every in-repo Android head.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  CircleAI.Inference.targets already knows how to register MNN as
  AndroidNativeLibrary — but it is imported by CircleAI.Inference.csproj
  ITSELF (see that csproj, ~line 72). An <Import> inside a csproj evaluates in
  THAT project's build, where TargetPlatformIdentifier is 'net10.0' and never
  'android'. So the Android block could never fire across a ProjectReference.
  It only works when the SDK is consumed as a NuGet package, where the targets
  land in the consumer's build context.

  The consequence, measured on a Huawei MAR-LX1M on 2026-07-20: the APK shipped
  with ZERO MNN libraries, the 433 MB model downloaded perfectly, and the first
  inference died with "System.DllNotFoundException: mnnbridge". A build-time
  omission surfacing as a runtime crash, on the SDK's headline capability.

  Directory.Build.targets is auto-imported by every project beneath this
  directory, so it evaluates in the ANDROID HEAD's context — which is the one
  place TargetPlatformIdentifier is actually 'android'.

  WHY ALL THE .so, NOT JUST TWO
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  CircleAI.Inference.targets lists only libmnnbridge.so + libMNN.so. The
  shipped runtime is eight files (libMNN_Express, libMNN_CL, libMNN_Vulkan,
  libmnncore, libMNNAudio, libMNNOpenCV as well). Bundling a partial set risks
  a dlopen failure on the dependency chain, so this globs the directory.

  WHY BUNDLE ON ANDROID AT ALL
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The SDK's default is runtime-fetch (CircleAIBundleMnn=false) — sensible on a
  server. On a phone it is not: it spends the user's mobile data on ~8 MB that
  compresses into the APK anyway, and dlopen from an app-data directory is far
  less reliable across Android versions than the OS-managed lib/ directory.
  So Android defaults to bundling here.

  OPTING OUT
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  An Android head that does not use on-device inference can set
  <CircleAIBundleMnn>false</CircleAIBundleMnn> and keep a thinner APK.

  THE SAME TRAP, A SECOND TIME: WHISPER
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Whisper.net.Runtime DOES ship Android natives, in build/android-arm64-v8a/.
  Its own .targets registers them as AndroidNativeLibrary — but gated on
  $(AndroidSupportedAbis), and that property is empty everywhere except an
  Android head. The package is referenced by CircleAI.Voice, a plain net10.0
  library, so the gate is evaluated there and never opens. Identical shape to
  the MNN story above: a native library that ships, is never bundled, and says
  nothing until a device tries to use it.

  Measured on the P30 on 2026-08-04, running the full voice loop for the first
  time: Talking worked, the ggml-tiny model downloaded and verified, the
  abilities screen said "Listening ✓ On" — and the loop died with
  "[voice] failed: libwhisper". The APK contained libaot-Whisper.net.dll.so,
  the MANAGED wrapper, and no libwhisper.so at all. An ability reporting itself
  as ON while its runtime is absent is the worst version of this bug, because
  the UI is confidently wrong.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android'">
    <!-- Bundle by default on Android. Reuses the SDK's existing knob name. -->
    <CircleAIBundleMnn Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == ''">true</CircleAIBundleMnn>
    <!-- src/CircleAI.Inference/ became src/CircleAI/Inference/ at 3.8.1. The old path
         survived the move, matched nothing, and the includes below are wildcards - so
         MNN silently stopped being bundled. The result was a 74 MB signed APK carrying
         291 native libraries and not one of them MNN: the exact nightmare the header of
         this file describes. Embeddings.Local and Voice carry natives too and were never
         covered here at all, so turbovec was equally absent. -->
    <_CircleAIMnnRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src/CircleAI/Inference/runtimes/</_CircleAIMnnRoot>
    <_CircleAITurboVecRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src/CircleAI/Embeddings.Local/runtimes/</_CircleAITurboVecRoot>
    <_CircleAIVoiceRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src/CircleAI/Voice/runtimes/</_CircleAIVoiceRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Wildcards that match nothing are silently empty, so an absent RID folder
    (e.g. android-x64) costs nothing and needs no Exists() guard.

    ONE GROUP PER ABI, EACH GATED ON THAT RID BEING DECLARED. Until 2026-10-28
    these four groups had no RID condition at all: every Android APK registered
    all four ABIs' natives whatever RuntimeIdentifiers said. The 32-bit APK
    therefore registered the whole arm64-v8a set and both Intel sets, and the
    census below counted all of them and printed one number over the top, so
    "Bundling 49 native lib(s)" said exactly as much about THIS APK as the
    "Bundling 291" that this file's own comment calls out twenty lines down. The
    Intel RIDs are gone from both APKs now, and an ungated group would have gone
    on registering x86_64 natives into an arm64-only package.

    The condition tests a ;-delimited token, not a substring: 'android-arm' IS a
    substring of 'android-arm64', so Contains('android-arm') is TRUE for a 64-bit
    APK and would re-add the armeabi-v7a set to it. Wrapping both sides in
    semicolons is what makes the match exact.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android' And '$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true'">
    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-arm64;'))"
                          Include="$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)android-arm64/native/*.so;$(_CircleAITurboVecRoot)android-arm64/native/*.so;$(_CircleAIVoiceRoot)android-arm64/native/*.so">
      <Abi>arm64-v8a</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>

    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-arm;'))"
                          Include="$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)android-arm/native/*.so;$(_CircleAITurboVecRoot)android-arm/native/*.so;$(_CircleAIVoiceRoot)android-arm/native/*.so">
      <Abi>armeabi-v7a</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>

    <!-- The two Intel groups are kept, gated and inert: no APK declares those
         RIDs since 2026-10-28, and neither root has ever held an Intel libMNN.so
         or libllamabridge.so. They stay so that adding the RID back is a
         one-property change AND so the gap is visible here rather than being
         rediscovered. android-x86 in particular had no MNN line here at all,
         while whisper registered x86 forty lines below - that asymmetry is what
         let lib/x86/ ship ears and no brain. -->
    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-x64;'))"
                          Include="$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)android-x64/native/*.so;$(_CircleAITurboVecRoot)android-x64/native/*.so;$(_CircleAIVoiceRoot)android-x64/native/*.so">
      <Abi>x86_64</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>

    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-x86;'))"
                          Include="$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)android-x86/native/*.so;$(_CircleAITurboVecRoot)android-x86/native/*.so;$(_CircleAIVoiceRoot)android-x86/native/*.so">
      <Abi>x86</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Whisper's natives, from wherever NuGet restored the runtime package. The
    path is resolved from the package reference rather than hard-coded, so a
    version bump does not silently stop bundling them.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android'">
    <CircleAIBundleWhisper Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleWhisper)' == ''">true</CircleAIBundleWhisper>
    <_WhisperRuntimeRoot Condition="'$(_WhisperRuntimeRoot)' == '' And '$(NuGetPackageRoot)' != ''">$(NuGetPackageRoot)whisper.net.runtime/1.8.1/build/</_WhisperRuntimeRoot>
    <_WhisperRuntimeRoot Condition="'$(_WhisperRuntimeRoot)' == ''">$(UserProfile)/.nuget/packages/whisper.net.runtime/1.8.1/build/</_WhisperRuntimeRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android' And '$(CircleAIBundleWhisper)' == 'true'">
    <!-- libwhisper.so needs its ggml siblings; bundling only the first is a
         dlopen failure one link further down the chain. Glob, do not list. -->
    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-arm64;'))"
                          Include="$(_WhisperRuntimeRoot)android-arm64-v8a/*.so">
      <Abi>arm64-v8a</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>

    <!-- Gated for the same reason as the MNN groups above: these two used to put
         libwhisper.so into lib/x86_64/ and lib/x86/ of EVERY APK, including the
         32-bit one, which is how an APK with no Intel engine still shipped Intel
         ears. There is no armeabi-v7a line because the package has no such
         folder - that gap is CIRCLEAI002 and it is still open. -->
    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-x64;'))"
                          Include="$(_WhisperRuntimeRoot)android-x86_64/*.so">
      <Abi>x86_64</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>

    <AndroidNativeLibrary Condition="$([System.String]::Concat(';','$(RuntimeIdentifiers)',';').Contains(';android-x86;'))"
                          Include="$(_WhisperRuntimeRoot)android-x86/*.so">
      <Abi>x86</Abi>
    </AndroidNativeLibrary>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Build-time visibility. The failure this replaces was invisible until first
    inference on a physical device, so say plainly what did or did not ship.
  -->
  <Target Name="_CircleAIReportAndroidNativeLibs"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android'">
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And '@(AndroidNativeLibrary)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] Bundling @(AndroidNativeLibrary->Count()) native lib(s) into the APK, for ABI(s) @(AndroidNativeLibrary->'%(Abi)'->Distinct(), ', ')." />

    <!--
      THESE THREE CHECKS NAME A FILE, BECAUSE THE ONE THEY REPLACE COUNTED A LIST.

      CIRCLEAI001 used to fire on '@(AndroidNativeLibrary)' == '' — the total
      across every family. Whisper and espeak populate that item unconditionally,
      so the total was never empty and the warning COULD NOT FIRE. That is not a
      hypothetical: the v3.8.1 APKs shipped 291 native libraries with zero MNN and
      zero turbovec, and this target printed a cheerful "Bundling 291 native
      lib(s)" over the top of it. A guard whose condition cannot be met is worse
      than no guard, because it reads like coverage.

      CIRCLEAI002 already had the right shape — !Exists() on one named file — and
      it is the reason the whisper gap was caught. Each family now gets the same,
      and each one names the symptom a person would actually see.
    -->
    <!--
      EVERY FAMILY, PER DECLARED ABI. THESE THREE NAMED android-arm64 AND NOTHING
      ELSE, SO A 32-BIT APK PASSED THEM ALL BY INTERROGATING A FOLDER IT DOES NOT
      SHIP. The remark below _CircleAIWarnAndroidAbiWithoutMnn said exactly that and
      nothing acted on it, so the v3.9.2 32-bit service shipped on 2026-10-28 with
      FOUR native families absent and every guard green. Measured on that artefact:

        lib/armeabi-v7a/   libMNN.so + 9 more          present
        lib/armeabi-v7a/   libllama, libllamabridge    ABSENT - no GGUF engine
        lib/armeabi-v7a/   libwhisper + ggml siblings  ABSENT - cannot transcribe
        lib/armeabi-v7a/   libespeak-ng                ABSENT - cannot phonemize
        lib/armeabi-v7a/   libturbovecbridge           ABSENT - no local embeddings

      The device said two of those out loud on first run - "gguf: NO BACKEND -
      libllamabridge.so did not load" and "phonemizer: in-process espeak failed" -
      while this target printed nothing at all. CIRCLEAI014 twenty lines down had
      the right shape the whole time: batch over RuntimeIdentifiers and name the ABI.
      These now do the same.

      A WARNING AND NOT AN ERROR, for the same reason CIRCLEAI014 is: no 32-bit
      binary exists for llama, whisper, espeak or turbovec anywhere in this tree,
      and whisper.net.runtime 1.8.1 ships no armeabi-v7a at all. Erroring would
      make the 32-bit cut unbuildable rather than honest. Whether to build those
      four for armeabi-v7a or stop shipping a 32-bit service is a product call;
      shipping it silently was not.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And '$(RuntimeIdentifiers)' != ''">
      <_CircleAIFamilyRid Include="$(RuntimeIdentifiers)" />
      <_CircleAIFamilyRid>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm64'">arm64-v8a</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm'">armeabi-v7a</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x64'">x86_64</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x86'">x86</Abi>
        <MnnFile>$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)%(Identity)/native/libMNN.so</MnnFile>
        <TurboVecFile>$(_CircleAITurboVecRoot)%(Identity)/native/libturbovecbridge.so</TurboVecFile>
        <EspeakFile>$(_CircleAIVoiceRoot)%(Identity)/native/libespeak-ng.so</EspeakFile>
        <GgmlBaseFile>$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)%(Identity)/native/libggml-base.so</GgmlBaseFile>
      </_CircleAIFamilyRid>

      <_CircleAINoMnn      Include="@(_CircleAIFamilyRid)" Condition="!Exists('%(_CircleAIFamilyRid.MnnFile)')" />
      <_CircleAINoTurboVec Include="@(_CircleAIFamilyRid)" Condition="!Exists('%(_CircleAIFamilyRid.TurboVecFile)')" />
      <_CircleAINoEspeak   Include="@(_CircleAIFamilyRid)" Condition="!Exists('%(_CircleAIFamilyRid.EspeakFile)')" />
      <_CircleAINoLlama    Include="@(_CircleAIFamilyRid)" Condition="!Exists('%(_CircleAIFamilyRid.GgmlBaseFile)')" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI001"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And '@(_CircleAINoMnn)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] No libMNN.so for ABI(s) @(_CircleAINoMnn->'%(Abi)', ', ') (looked under $(_CircleAIMnnRoot)&lt;rid&gt;/native/). On-device inference fails at runtime with DllNotFoundException: mnnbridge on those ABIs, while the abilities screen still offers model downloads." />
    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI003"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And '@(_CircleAINoTurboVec)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] No libturbovecbridge.so for ABI(s) @(_CircleAINoTurboVec->'%(Abi)', ', '). Local embeddings and semantic recall fail on those ABIs; retrieval silently returns nothing rather than erroring." />
    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI004"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And '@(_CircleAINoEspeak)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] No libespeak-ng.so for ABI(s) @(_CircleAINoEspeak->'%(Abi)', ', '). The device logs 'phonemizer: in-process espeak failed - libespeak-ng not found' and falls back to a separate app, so the assistant cannot speak its answer on those ABIs." />

    <!--
      THE GGUF ENGINE, AND ITS WHOLE DEPENDENCY CHAIN. libllamabridge.so is a thin
      shim: it NEEDS libllama, libggml, libggml-cpu and libggml-base, and bundling
      the shim alone is a dlopen failure one link further down - the exact mistake
      the header of this file warns about for whisper's ggml siblings.
      So the check names the DEEPEST dependency, not the shim: libggml-base is the
      leaf, and if it is present the four above it were staged in the same copy.
      libllamabridge is checked too, because it is the one a p/invoke names and the
      one whose absence logs "Shared library 'llamabridge' not loaded".
      These were built on 2026-09-28 into native/llama-bridge/build-android/, which
      is gitignored, and sat there unstaged until 2026-10-09 - built, working, and
      never shipped. Same shape as the MNN bug, found the same way: by reading what
      a device actually said.
    -->
    <!--
      NOW PER ABI, AND NO LONGER CONDITIONAL ON THE SHIM BEING PRESENT. The old
      condition only fired when libllamabridge WAS staged and libggml-base was not -
      so an ABI with NEITHER (armeabi-v7a, which has no llama build at all) slipped
      through in silence. That is the case that actually shipped.
    -->
    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI005"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And '@(_CircleAINoLlama)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] No libggml-base.so for ABI(s) @(_CircleAINoLlama->'%(Abi)', ', '), so the GGUF engine is absent there. The device logs 'gguf: NO BACKEND - libllamabridge.so did not load. A .gguf model cannot be opened on this device.' and every PTQ1_0 catalogue entry becomes unusable with no stated reason. Build libllama.so, libggml.so, libggml-cpu.so, libggml-base.so and libllamabridge.so for that ABI into native/llama-bridge/build-android/bin/ and stage them under $(_CircleAIMnnRoot)&lt;rid&gt;/native/." />
    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI005B"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true' And Exists('$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)android-arm64/native/libllamabridge.so') And !Exists('$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)android-arm64/native/libggml-base.so')"
             Text="[CircleAI] libllamabridge.so is staged but libggml-base.so is not. The GGUF engine will fail at dlopen with a missing dependency rather than reporting no backend - LlamaInterop.TryGetVersion returns null and the catalogue's PTQ1_0 chat model becomes unusable with no stated reason. Copy libllama.so, libggml.so, libggml-cpu.so and libggml-base.so from native/llama-bridge/build-android/bin/." />

    <!--
      ONLY A PROJECT THAT ACTUALLY BUILDS AN APK MAY SAY ANYTHING ABOUT ONE.
      This message used to fire on every android build of the LIBRARY, which
      produces no APK, announcing "no MNN in the APK" while the head's APK
      carried eight MNN libraries. After the real MNN bug, a false reassurance in
      the opposite direction is the last thing this file should emit.

      WHY THE LIBRARY READS false AND THE HEAD READS true - one fact, two owners:
        Directory.Build.targets:67            defaults it to TRUE  (android)
        src/CircleAI/Inference/
          CircleAI.Inference.targets:75       defaults it to FALSE (any platform)
      Both are guarded on '== null or empty', so the FIRST evaluation wins.
      CircleAI.csproj <Import>s Inference.targets in its body, which is evaluated
      before Directory.Build.targets is imported at the end - so the library gets
      false. CircleAIService only project-references the library, never imports
      that file, so it gets true. Measured with -getProperty:CircleAIBundleMnn,
      not inferred.
      Both defaults are deliberate: false is the SHIPPED default for NuGet
      consumers ("fetch at runtime, sensible on a server"), true is the
      repo-local default for our own android heads. The bug was never the values,
      it was a library being asked about an APK.
    -->
    <!--
      AndroidApplication, NOT OutputType. This condition said OutputType == 'Exe'
      until 2026-10-25 and therefore could never be met: measured with
      -getProperty on both projects at net10.0-android, OutputType is 'Library'
      for the APK head AND for the library, so it cannot tell them apart and is
      never 'Exe' at all. The paragraph above is right about WHY the guard needs
      a discriminator and wrong about which one works.

        CircleAIService.csproj  OutputType=Library  AndroidApplication=true
        CircleAI.csproj         OutputType=Library  AndroidApplication=false

      The reassurance this prints was unreachable, which is the same defect as
      the CIRCLEAI001 it sits under: a condition that cannot be met reads like
      coverage. It stayed invisible because the other half of the AND
      (CircleAIBundleMnn != 'true') is also false on the head, so nothing was
      missing from any log.
    -->
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android' And '$(AndroidApplication)' == 'true' And '$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' != 'true'"
             Text="[CircleAI] CircleAIBundleMnn=false — no MNN in this APK. On-device inference requires a runtime fetch." />

    <!-- Whisper gets its OWN check. Counting all native libraries together
         would let a full set of MNN mask a completely absent whisper, which is
         exactly how this shipped: 163 libraries in the APK and not one of them
         the one the ears needed. -->
    <!--
      WHISPER PER ABI TOO, AND THE PACKAGE'S FOLDER NAMES ARE NOT THE RIDs:
      whisper.net.runtime/1.8.1/build/ ships android-arm64-v8a, android-x86 and
      android-x86_64 - and NO armeabi-v7a. So a 32-bit APK cannot carry libwhisper
      at all from this package, and the old arm64-only check reported nothing.
      That is why the 32-bit service installs, spots the wake word through the
      zipformer, and then cannot transcribe the question.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleWhisper)' == 'true' And '$(RuntimeIdentifiers)' != ''">
      <_CircleAIWhisperRid Include="$(RuntimeIdentifiers)" />
      <_CircleAIWhisperRid>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm64'">arm64-v8a</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm'">armeabi-v7a</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x64'">x86_64</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x86'">x86</Abi>
        <Folder Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm64'">android-arm64-v8a</Folder>
        <Folder Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm'">android-armeabi-v7a</Folder>
        <Folder Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x64'">android-x86_64</Folder>
        <Folder Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x86'">android-x86</Folder>
      </_CircleAIWhisperRid>
      <_CircleAINoWhisper Include="@(_CircleAIWhisperRid)"
                          Condition="!Exists('$(_WhisperRuntimeRoot)%(_CircleAIWhisperRid.Folder)/libwhisper.so')" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI002"
             Condition="'$(CircleAIBundleWhisper)' == 'true' And '@(_CircleAINoWhisper)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] No libwhisper.so for ABI(s) @(_CircleAINoWhisper->'%(Abi)', ', ') under $(_WhisperRuntimeRoot). Speech recognition fails on device with 'failed: libwhisper' while the abilities screen still reports Listening as ON. whisper.net.runtime 1.8.1 ships arm64-v8a, x86 and x86_64 only - there is no armeabi-v7a in the package, so a 32-bit APK needs whisper built from source." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    EVERY ABI THIS APK DECLARES, NOT THE ONE WE HAPPEN TO TEST ON.

    CIRCLEAI001/003/004 above each name a file, which is the right shape — but
    all three name it under android-arm64, and an APK declares TWO RIDs:

      CircleAIService.csproj:87   CircleAIAbi=32  ->  android-arm
      CircleAIService.csproj:88   otherwise       ->  android-arm64

    So on a 32-bit build those three guards interrogate android-arm64 — a folder
    this build does not ship — and pass for a reason that has nothing to do with
    the APK being produced. armeabi-v7a's MNN set is complete today by the luck
    of that folder existing, not because anything checked it.

    Each APK declares ONE RID since 2026-10-28; it declared two until then, and
    the batching below stays RID-driven anyway. A guard that reads
    $(RuntimeIdentifiers) keeps telling the truth when somebody adds a RID back;
    a guard that hard-codes android-arm64 starts lying the moment they do.

    And on BOTH builds the x86 half shipped with no LLM engine and nothing said
    so. Measured on the v3.9.2 artefacts, 2026-10-25:

      service-32  lib/armeabi-v7a/  libMNN.so + libmnnbridge.so + 8 more   OK
      service-32  lib/x86/          libwhisper + libonnxruntime, NO MNN
      service-64  lib/arm64-v8a/    libMNN.so + libmnnbridge.so + 8 more   OK
      service-64  lib/x86_64/       NO MNN

    An x86 Android emulator therefore starts, binds, downloads a model and dies
    at the first load — the identical shape as the win-x86 publish that
    CIRCLEAI013 now covers, and invisible here for the same reason: the wildcard
    for an absent RID folder is silently empty. The comment at the top of this
    file calls that out as costing nothing, and for the BUILD it costs nothing.
    It is the APK that pays.

    No physical device in the roster is x86, so this was emulator-only. It is a
    warning and not an error for exactly that reason: the non-inference half of
    the service works there, and a thinner APK is a product decision, not a
    build failure.

    THAT DECISION WAS TAKEN ON 2026-10-28: both Intel RIDs are gone from both
    APKs, which is the "removing that RID" half of the fix this warning names.
    So the x86 rows above are history, not a live defect — the guard now has
    nothing Intel to fire on, and the only ABI it still reports is armeabi-v7a,
    whose own gaps (turbovecbridge, espeak-ng, whisper) are real and open.

    This extinguishes itself the day somebody builds MNN for Android x86: the
    registration above picks the files up and this warning stops firing.
  -->
  <Target Name="_CircleAIWarnAndroidAbiWithoutMnn"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android'
                     And '$(AndroidApplication)' == 'true'
                     And '$(CircleAIBundleMnn)' == 'true'
                     And '$(RuntimeIdentifiers)' != ''">
    <ItemGroup>
      <!-- RuntimeIdentifiers is semicolon-separated, so Include splits it. -->
      <_CircleAIAbiRid Include="$(RuntimeIdentifiers)" />
      <_CircleAIAbiRid>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm64'">arm64-v8a</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-arm'">armeabi-v7a</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x64'">x86_64</Abi>
        <Abi Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'android-x86'">x86</Abi>
        <MnnFile>$(_CircleAIMnnRoot)%(Identity)/native/libMNN.so</MnnFile>
      </_CircleAIAbiRid>
      <_CircleAIAbiNoMnn Include="@(_CircleAIAbiRid)" Condition="!Exists('%(_CircleAIAbiRid.MnnFile)')" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="[CircleAI] APK ABIs: @(_CircleAIAbiRid->'%(Abi)', ', ') — MNN present for @(_CircleAIAbiRid->Count()) RID(s) minus @(_CircleAIAbiNoMnn->Count()) without." />

    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI014"
             Condition="'@(_CircleAIAbiNoMnn)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] This APK declares ABI(s) @(_CircleAIAbiNoMnn->'%(Abi)', ', ') with no libMNN.so staged for them (looked for @(_CircleAIAbiNoMnn->'%(MnnFile)', ' and ')). On a device or emulator of that ABI the service starts, binds and downloads models, then fails at the first model load — on-device inference is UNAVAILABLE there. Everything in the service that is not inference still works. No MNN prebuilt exists for Android x86/x86_64 in this tree; building one, or removing that RID from RuntimeIdentifiers, are the two ways to clear this." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    GLIBC NATIVES MUST NOT REACH AN ANDROID APK.

    Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime ships Android support as an AAR, and separately
    ships runtimes/linux-arm64/native/libonnxruntime.so for desktop Linux. The
    .NET RID graph lets android-arm64 fall back through linux-bionic-arm64 to
    linux-arm64, so NuGet offers that LINUX file to an ANDROID build. Both are
    named libonnxruntime.so and both want lib/arm64-v8a/, so the RID-resolved
    one overwrote the AAR's and the APK shipped a binary needing libc.so.6 and
    libdl.so.2 - glibc names that do not exist on bionic. It cannot load:

        System.DllNotFoundException: libonnxruntime.so

    Measured on a P30 2026-09-02: the wake word could not start in the hybrid
    head, and had never been able to. The AAR's own library is fine (19.1 MB,
    needs libc.so / libdl.so / libm.so) and takes over once the impostor is gone.

    Only packages whose Android build arrives as an AAR are exposed to this.
    SourceGear.sqlite3 ships a real runtimes/android-*/native/ folder, so its
    Android asset wins its own name - libe_sqlite3.so is correctly bionic in the
    same APK, which is why SQLite worked while ONNX did not.

    linux-bionic-* is deliberately NOT filtered: those RIDs ARE Android.
  -->
  <Target Name="CircleAIDropGlibcNativesFromAndroid"
          Condition="'$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android'"
          AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList"
          BeforeTargets="_CopyFilesMarkedCopyLocal;_BuildApkFastDev;_CreateBaseApk;ProcessNativeLibraries">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_CircleAIGlibcNative Include="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
                            Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Contains('/runtimes/linux-')) And !$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Contains('/runtimes/linux-bionic'))" />
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(_CircleAIGlibcNative)" />
      <RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems Remove="@(RuntimeTargetsCopyLocalItems)"
                                    Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Contains('/runtimes/linux-')) And !$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Contains('/runtimes/linux-bionic'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'@(_CircleAIGlibcNative)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] Dropped @(_CircleAIGlibcNative->Count()) glibc native(s) that the RID graph offered to this Android build." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    A RELEASE APK THAT IS NOT SIGNED WITH THE SHARED KEY IS NOT A FAILING BUILD.
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Directory.Build.props already explains the hazard at length: with
    AndroidKeyStore unset the SDK's _ResolveAndroidSigningKey falls back to the
    platform debug keystore, and you get a Release APK that installs, runs, passes
    every suite, and can be shipped nowhere. The explanation was written on
    2026-10-01 and nothing enforced it.

    So on 2026-10-02 and 2026-10-03, with the block in place and
    ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASS correctly unset on the dev box, two Release APKs were
    built and both were wrong - and both builds exited 0:

      com.bhengubv.circleai.hybrid-Signed.apk    debug certificate (O=Android, C=US)
      com.bhengubv.circleai.service-Signed.apk   DOES NOT VERIFY - no META-INF/MANIFEST.MF

    The "-Signed" in the filename is not a claim about the signer. Only what
    apksigner reports for the certificate is; the exact command is in the Message
    at the bottom of this target, where a flag may legally contain two hyphens.

    THREE WAYS THIS GUARD WOULD SILENTLY DO NOTHING, all hit before:
      - BeforeTargets="Build" runs AFTER $(BuildDependsOn), which already contains
        the signing. Hence PrepareForBuild.
      - Hooking _ResolveAndroidSigningKey does not fire at all.
      - '$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' is FALSE for an Android app: the csproj says Exe
        and the SDK rewrites it to Library. Gate on AndroidApplication, which is
        execution-time only - which is also why this lives in a Target and cannot
        be a PropertyGroup condition in Directory.Build.props.

    The password is never an environment variable on a dev box. It is read per
    invocation from thegeeknetwork\Deployment\deployment-credentials.ps1
    ($SIGNING_CREDENTIALS.ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD) and passed as -p:, or Visual
    Studio's Archive flow supplies it from TheGeekAlias.keyInfo. Debug builds never
    reach here, so nothing blocks testing.
  -->
  <Target Name="_CircleAIRefuseUnsignedRelease"
          BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild"
          Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' == 'android'
                     And '$(AndroidApplication)' == 'true'">

    <Error Code="CIRCLEAI010"
           Condition="'$(AndroidKeyStore)' != 'true'"
           Text="[CircleAI] Release Android build with AndroidKeyStore != true. The SDK would fall back to the platform DEBUG keystore and still exit 0, producing an APK that installs and runs and can be shipped nowhere. Build Debug, or supply the signing properties." />

    <Error Code="CIRCLEAI011"
           Condition="'$(AndroidSigningKeyPass)' == '' Or '$(AndroidSigningStorePass)' == ''"
           Text="[CircleAI] Release Android build with no signing password, so the APK would come out debug-signed or with no MANIFEST.MF. Pass it for this invocation only - read $SIGNING_CREDENTIALS.ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD from thegeeknetwork\Deployment\deployment-credentials.ps1 and add -p:AndroidSigningKeyPass=... -p:AndroidSigningStorePass=... - or use Visual Studio's Archive flow. Never setx it." />

    <Error Code="CIRCLEAI012"
           Condition="'$(AndroidSigningKeyStore)' != '' And !Exists('$(AndroidSigningKeyStore)')"
           Text="[CircleAI] Release Android build points at a keystore that is not there: '$(AndroidSigningKeyStore)'. Canonical copy lives under %LOCALAPPDATA%\Xamarin\Mono for Android\Keystore\TheGeekAlias\." />

    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="[CircleAI] Release APK will be signed with alias '$(AndroidSigningKeyAlias)'. Verify the artefact with: apksigner verify --print-certs &lt;apk&gt;" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    A RID-SPECIFIC DESKTOP PUBLISH SHIPS EVERY OTHER PLATFORM'S NATIVES.
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    CircleAI.csproj copies the native payload with one glob over
    Inference/runtimes/**, Embeddings.Local/runtimes/** and Voice/runtimes/**.
    That glob is deliberate and load-bearing - deleting it once left the build
    green and twelve tests dying on "DllNotFoundException: mnnbridge" - but it
    has no RID filter, because a NuGet PACKAGE wants every RID in it.

    A publish is not a package. Measured 2026-10-24, `publish -r win-x86` and
    `publish -r win-x64` of CircleAIService came out at 231 MB and 233 MB, and
    the two directories carried an IDENTICAL runtimes/ tree: 14 RIDs including
    android-arm64's libllama.so at 46 MB, both macOS dylibs, the iOS static
    bridge, and the Linux .so set. None of it can load in a Windows process.

    The win-x86 publish was the one that mattered. It shipped
    runtimes/win-x64/native/MNN.dll - 8.3 MB of 64-bit code under a name the
    resolver is looking for - so the artefact LOOKED complete. There is no
    win-x86 MNN in the tree and none upstream to put there (see CIRCLEAI013),
    and a person debugging that service would have found a file called MNN.dll
    sitting exactly where it belongs and drawn the wrong conclusion.

    NuGet's own RID assets need no help here: the SDK flattens the matching
    RID to the publish root and drops the rest, which is why libsodium.dll and
    onnxruntime.dll in the x86 publish are the x86 builds. Only the plain
    content copies above survive, so only they need filtering.

    PUBLISH ONLY. This touches ResolvedFileToPublish and nothing else - bin/
    still gets every RID, so the test legs keep the host native they P/Invoke
    and `dotnet pack` keeps all 14 in the package.

    One sharp edge: the match is textual on the RID folder name, so a publish
    using a NON-PORTABLE RID (win10-x64 rather than win-x64) would see
    runtimes/win-x64/ as foreign and drop it. The prefix guard below keeps this
    target off anything that is not a plain win-/linux-/osx- RID, and the
    Message names every file removed so an over-eager drop is visible in the
    log rather than discovered in the field.
  -->
  <Target Name="CircleAIDropForeignNativesFromDesktopPublish"
          AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList"
          Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' != 'android'
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' != 'ios'
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' != 'maccatalyst'
                     And ( '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'win-x64'   Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'win-x86'
                        Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'win-arm64'
                        Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'linux-x64' Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'linux-arm64'
                        Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'osx-x64'   Or '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == 'osx-arm64' )">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_CircleAIForeignNative
          Include="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
          Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(RelativePath)').Replace('\','/').Contains('runtimes/'))
                     And !$([System.String]::Copy('%(RelativePath)').Replace('\','/').Contains('runtimes/$(RuntimeIdentifier)/'))" />
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(_CircleAIForeignNative)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Message Importance="high"
             Condition="'@(_CircleAIForeignNative)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI] Publish is $(RuntimeIdentifier): dropped @(_CircleAIForeignNative->Count()) native(s) belonging to other RIDs." />
    <Message Importance="normal"
             Condition="'@(_CircleAIForeignNative)' != ''"
             Text="[CircleAI]   dropped: %(_CircleAIForeignNative.RelativePath)" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    A DESKTOP PUBLISH WITH NO MNN BRIDGE FOR ITS OWN RID CANNOT DO INFERENCE.
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The repo carries the native payload per RID under
    src/CircleAI/Inference/runtimes/{RID}/native/. On 2026-10-24 that was
    win-x64, linux-x64, linux-arm64, osx-x64, osx-arm64, android-arm,
    android-arm64 and ios-arm64 - and nothing else. A `publish -r win-x86`
    therefore produces a service that starts, serves, downloads models, and
    then cannot load an engine.

    Nothing said so. The six-artefact set built that day included a 231 MB
    32-bit Windows service whose first model load was always going to fail,
    and the build exited 0 with no warning of any kind. That is the same shape
    of defect as an unsigned Release APK, which is what CIRCLEAI010-012 above
    exist to catch, so it gets a code in the same band.

    WHY THIS IS A WARNING AND NOT AN ERROR. A win-x86 MNN cannot simply be
    added: Alibaba publishes no 32-bit Windows bundle in ANY release (checked
    3.3.0 through 3.6.1 - every Windows asset is windows_x64), MNN's source is
    not vendored here, and native/mnn-bridge builds the shim against a
    prebuilt bundle. So the fix is an MNN source build for Win32, not a file
    copy, and failing the build would only block a deliberate 32-bit publish
    that someone may still want for the non-inference half of the service.

    The day a win-x86 MNN lands in the tree this warning stops by itself,
    because it checks the tree rather than a hardcoded list of RIDs.
  -->
  <Target Name="_CircleAIWarnNoInferenceForRid"
          BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild"
          Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''
                     And '$(OutputType)' == 'Exe'
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' != 'android'
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' != 'ios'
                     And '$(TargetPlatformIdentifier)' != 'maccatalyst'">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_CircleAIBridgeName Condition="$(RuntimeIdentifier.StartsWith('win'))">mnnbridge.dll</_CircleAIBridgeName>
      <_CircleAIBridgeName Condition="$(RuntimeIdentifier.StartsWith('osx'))">libmnnbridge.dylib</_CircleAIBridgeName>
      <_CircleAIBridgeName Condition="'$(_CircleAIBridgeName)' == ''">libmnnbridge.so</_CircleAIBridgeName>
      <_CircleAIBridgePath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)src/CircleAI/Inference/runtimes/$(RuntimeIdentifier)/native/$(_CircleAIBridgeName)</_CircleAIBridgePath>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Warning Code="CIRCLEAI013"
             Condition="!Exists('$(_CircleAIBridgePath)')"
             Text="[CircleAI] No $(_CircleAIBridgeName) for $(RuntimeIdentifier) at $(_CircleAIBridgePath). This publish will start, serve and download models, then fail on the first model load - on-device inference is UNAVAILABLE in it. For win-x86 specifically there is no upstream fix: Alibaba ships no 32-bit Windows MNN bundle in any release, so an MNN source build for Win32 is required. Everything in the service that is not inference still works." />
  </Target>

</Project>
